# Hostile audit — finite-window switching-direction collapse through `t<=1.86596`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Reviewed:**
- `research/target_c_finite_window_direction_collapse_t186596_2026-09-15.md`
- `research/verify_target_c_finite_window_defect_strip_t186596.py`

**Verdict:** **PASS at proof-only full-core Type-II scope.**  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The candidate adds the outer-window ledger to the finite-defect/KKT average and then
uses global source-hull disk convergence to collapse switching diameter directions. The
load-bearing issues are whether the window lengths really add globally, whether actual
diameter endpoints lie in those windows, and whether endpoint collapse determines the
orientation of the antipodal diameter partner.

## 1. The middle boundary windows are disjoint in occurrence space

The selected O--O crosscuts are pairwise disjoint proper crosscuts and form one simple
complement-dual path. Consecutive crosscuts cut out linearly ordered middle regions.
Away from crosscut endpoints, the outer-boundary traces of distinct middle regions are
disjoint on the occurrence circle. Therefore

\[
\sum_iG_i\le b
\]

holds even when one physical bridge appears more than once on the outer facial walk:
its two occurrences are distinct points of the occurrence circle. Endpoint half-edge
ties have zero effect on the total metric length.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 2. The high-`t` outer-walk bound is scope-safe

The distinguished-route identity

\[
b/D=\pi+\delta+o(1)
\]

is independent of the path extraction. The paired-remainder branch already uses the
matched `g=.0076` residual envelope

\[
2\delta+\kappa/2+h=9t/2-2\pi-C_{76},
\qquad \kappa,h\ge0.
\]

Thus the adverse `delta` is obtained by setting `t=T=1.86596` and
`kappa=h=0`. The exact verifier recomputes `C76`, `pi`, and the square root with outward
rational bounds and proves

\[
\delta<0.100705,
\qquad
(\pi+\delta)<3.2423+o(1).
\]

No stronger `.007707` gap from a different branch is imported.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 3. All four costs are averaged on the same eligible regions

For the nonhub internal path regions,

\[
\sum_iN_i\le2\pi D,
\quad
\sum_iL_i\le(10T-4\pi)D+o(D),
\quad
\sum_iDB_i\le2D,
\quad
\sum_iG_i\le(\pi+\delta_{max})D+o(D).
\]

All quantities are nonnegative and indexed by the same path regions. Summing before
averaging is therefore legitimate; no separate favorable strips are combined.

The exact certificate proves

\[
{10T-\pi+2+\delta_{max}\over17/10^6}<1{,}036{,}395.
\]

Deleting one hub region and two end regions changes only `O(1)` terms, hence the rounded
finite conclusion `<1,036,400` holds eventually.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 4. Switching middle endpoints are genuinely in the two windows

Signed interval support for adjacent cuts gives exactly one middle-layer endpoint for
each switching diameter edge. Every endpoint of an actual diameter pair is an extreme
point of the full source convex hull; the standard strict-convexity argument excludes a
nontrivial convex combination endpoint at distance `D`. Thus the middle endpoint occurs
on the actual outer boundary and lies on one of the two middle-region boundary arcs
`J_i^+,J_i^-`.

For source vertices in one outer-walk interval, Euclidean distance is at most the
outer-walk path length. Since each window has length at most their fixed total, each
window's switching middle endpoints occupy a set of Euclidean diameter `O(1)=o(D)`.

If both left- and right-switch rows occur, the hostile-audited switching-order theorem
places every participating middle endpoint on one common middle boundary arc. At the
adjacent-strip level that arc is one of `J_i^+,J_i^-`; it is not a union of the two.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 5. Global source-hull disk convergence is sufficient

For any sequence of global minimum-diameter packings,

\[
n/D^2\to\pi/(2\sqrt3).
\]

Finite Oler plus `per(K)<=pi D` gives

\[
\liminf area(K)/D^2\ge\pi/4,
\]

while the isodiametric inequality gives the reverse upper bound. Hence normalized
source hull area tends to `pi/4`. Blaschke compactness and uniqueness of equality in the
planar isodiametric theorem force every translated Hausdorff limit to be the radius
`1/2` disk.

The proof needs the **full source hull**, not the force hull, so contact-isolated points
do not create a scope gap.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 6. A short first-endpoint patch fixes the oriented diameter direction

Take two actual diameter pairs oriented toward first endpoints `x_D,x'_D` with
`|x_D-x'_D|=o(D)`. After scaling and taking a convergent subsequence the first endpoints
have the same disk limit `x`. Their opposite endpoints `y,y'` lie in the radius-`1/2`
disk and satisfy

\[
|x-y|=|x-y'|=1.
\]

The only disk point at distance one from a given disk point is its antipode, so
`y=y'`. Hence the **oriented** normalized diameter vectors converge together. This is
stronger than projective collapse and justifies (FW.23)--(FW.24).

The uniform family version follows by the usual contradiction/subsequence argument;
no rate is claimed.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 7. The mixed-switch reaction inherits the common ray

In the mixed case all layer-oriented switching vectors satisfy

\[
d_h=\nu_D+o(1)
\]

uniformly. With positive switching KKT multipliers and
`B=sum_h beta_h>0`,

\[
{G\over DB}={1\over B}\sum_h\beta_hd_h=\nu_D+o(1).
\]

The right side has norm tending to one, so normalizing `G` does not amplify an error:

\[
G/|G|-\nu_D\to0.
\]

This remains valid even if `B=O(1/D)` or much smaller. No lower bound on switching KKT
mass is required.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 8. What is not proved

The theorem gives only qualitative `o(1)` direction collapse. It does not give an
`O(1/D)` angular rate, a uniform inactive-slack scale, or a finite deformation that
bypasses the remaining ray. In the one-orientation case two endpoint windows and two
limiting rays may remain. In the mixed case one ray remains, but that ray can still be
strictly first-order load-bearing at every finite `D`.

**Final verdict:** **PASS.** One `t<=1.86596` nonhub strip is simultaneously
finite-defect, low-KKT, fixed-window, and at-most-two-ray; mixed switching collapses to
one oriented diameter ray and the exact KKT reaction follows that ray.
